## Claim
Text: "Twitter now putting warnings on tweets that are just unedited clips of Biden saying he would end fracking"
Claim author: Zach Parkinson
Claim date: October 23, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### In October 2020, did Twitter have an official policy of applying warning labels or contextual information to tweets containing video clips of political figures?
Answer: The search result describes a policy announced in June 2019 in which Twitter added warning labels to tweets from verified political leaders and candidates that violated the platform's rules on abusive behavior, stating: "The Twitter Rules about abusive behavior apply to this Tweet. However, Twitter has determined it may be in the public's interest for the Tweet to remain available." However, this policy was specifically aimed at tweets containing offensive, bullying, or hateful content that broke Twitter's rules—not at labeling or suppressing tweets containing ordinary video clips of political figures speaking. The search result does not indicate that Twitter had a policy of applying warning labels or contextual information to unedited video clips of politicians simply making statements. Therefore, the information here does not fully answer the question as it relates to the specific claim, but it does confirm that Twitter had a precedent for applying labels to political tweets under certain conditions.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2019/06/27/736668003/twitter-adds-warning-label-for-offensive-political-tweets

### What was Twitter's specific policy regarding the labeling of "synthetic and manipulated media" versus "unmanipulated media" in October 2020?
Answer: According to Twitter's policy announced in February 2020 and active in October 2020, the company would label content as "synthetic and manipulated media" if it was "altered or fabricated" and likely to cause harm or deceive; it applied a three-part test to determine this. The policy specifically targeted manipulated media, including both deepfakes and low-tech "cheapfakes," but did not address or label "unmanipulated media" such as unedited video clips.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html

