Answer: Yes, Twitter publicly explained its rationale through its safety blog, stating that users wanted more context for "non-specific, disputed information that could cause confusion about an election," and that the policy aimed to label content "intended to undermine public confidence in an election or other civic process."

Source URL: https://blog.twitter.com/en_us/topics/company/2020/civic-integrity-policy-update

### What was the official distinction in Twitter's 2020 policies between "misleading information" and "unverified claims" when applied to statements made by political candidates?
Answer: According to Twitter's 2020 Civic Integrity Policy, the official distinction was: **"Misleading Information"** referred to "statements or assertions that have been confirmed to be false or misleading by subject-matter experts," while **"Unverified Claims"** were "information (which could be true or false) that is unconfirmed at the time it is shared."

